    "plan_strategy": "Attempt 1: replace the linear baseline with a gradient-boosted tree ensemble (hist_gbdt) to raise the ranking itself, and pair it with an explicit positive-class weight so the default predict() cut lands near the balanced point. Configuration: hist_gbdt with max_iter=400, learning_rate=0.06, max_leaf_nodes=31, max_depth=None, l2_regularization=1.0, early_stopping=True with validation_fraction=0.1 and n_iter_no_change=25 so the iteration count self-limits inside the 600 s budget. Preprocessing: impute='none' so the trees split on NaN in workclass/occupation/native-country directly (these are one-hot encoded categoricals with ~5.7% missing), scale=false (trees are scale-invariant, and fnlwgt/capital-gain skew does not matter to split finding), and no missing_indicator (documented as bit-for-bit redundant under impute='none') and no missing_count (bought nothing in the recorded measurements, and here missingness is a survey non-response, not a recording-regime marker). Imbalance: class_weight is not accepted by HistGradientBoostingClassifier's set_params in all versions, so I pass class_weight='balanced' and expect it to apply where supported; if it is dropped it will show in dropped_hyperparams and attempt 2 will move the operating point instead. Then read, in this order: balanced_accuracy_at_best_cut (the new ranking ceiling — the bar 0.8248 sits above the baseline's 0.8238 ceiling, so this number is the gate), cut_headroom (which axis the remaining gap is on), roc_auc/pr_auc against 0.9075/0.7706, and recall vs specificity to see which side the cut is biased toward. If the new ceiling clears 0.8248 but the achieved score does not, attempt 2 tunes only the weight (explicit {\"0\":1,\"1\":2.5}-style map) on this same ranking; if the ceiling itself falls short, attempt 2 switches ranking generator (xgboost with deeper trees, or random_forest) rather than touching the cut."
